1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,273
Bread dough left in a warm place puffs up.

2
00:00:03,273 --> 00:00:06,536
A tub of batter rises
overnight, full of bubbles.

3
00:00:06,536 --> 00:00:09,603
After a hard sprint,
your legs ache and burn.

4
00:00:10,003 --> 00:00:11,497
Three different places.

5
00:00:11,497 --> 00:00:14,258
One molecule behind
them all: pyruvic acid.

6
00:00:14,708 --> 00:00:16,709
Diya noticed the batter in the kitchen.

7
00:00:16,709 --> 00:00:18,947
She asked: where do the bubbles come from?

8
00:00:18,947 --> 00:00:22,215
And what does a cell do with
pyruvic acid when oxygen runs short?

9
00:00:22,565 --> 00:00:24,096
Here is the short answer.

10
00:00:24,096 --> 00:00:26,530
Without oxygen, cells
ferment pyruvic acid.

11
00:00:26,530 --> 00:00:30,435
With oxygen, they burn it fully in a
wheel of reactions called the Krebs

12
00:00:30,435 --> 00:00:31,335
cycle.

13
00:00:31,454 --> 00:00:36,350
By the end, you will know alcoholic and
lactic acid fermentation, how pyruvic

14
00:00:36,350 --> 00:00:40,523
acid enters the mitochondria,
and the Krebs cycle step by step.

15
00:00:41,173 --> 00:00:43,416
First, remember where we left off.

16
00:00:43,766 --> 00:00:48,040
Glycolysis split one glucose into
two pyruvic acid, and made two NADH.

17
00:00:48,490 --> 00:00:50,721
Now pyruvic acid has two paths.

18
00:00:50,721 --> 00:00:53,830
Without oxygen, fermentation
in the cytoplasm.

19
00:00:53,830 --> 00:00:57,363
With oxygen, aerobic
respiration in the mitochondria.

20
00:00:58,013 --> 00:00:59,918
Let's see fermentation first.

21
00:01:00,268 --> 00:01:03,901
In yeast, pyruvic acid becomes
carbon dioxide and ethanol.

22
00:01:03,901 --> 00:01:07,966
The enzymes are pyruvic acid
decarboxylase and alcohol dehydrogenase.

23
00:01:07,966 --> 00:01:10,766
Those carbon dioxide
bubbles make dough rise.

24
00:01:11,216 --> 00:01:13,903
Some bacteria make lactic acid instead.

25
00:01:13,903 --> 00:01:18,647
Our muscles do this too, when oxygen
runs short during hard exercise.

26
00:01:18,647 --> 00:01:21,113
The enzyme is lactate dehydrogenase.

27
00:01:21,113 --> 00:01:26,182
In both kinds, NADH gives its hydrogen
back, and becomes NAD plus again.

28
00:01:26,832 --> 00:01:28,941
But fermentation is a poor deal.

29
00:01:29,291 --> 00:01:32,937
Less than seven percent of the
energy in glucose is released.

30
00:01:33,387 --> 00:01:36,486
And the products are
harmful, acid or alcohol.

31
00:01:36,486 --> 00:01:40,683
Yeasts poison themselves when alcohol
reaches about thirteen percent.

32
00:01:41,283 --> 00:01:42,965
Now the oxygen path.

33
00:01:42,965 --> 00:01:44,185
Let's zoom in.

34
00:01:44,535 --> 00:01:46,060
Here is a plant cell.

35
00:01:46,060 --> 00:01:49,824
Pyruvic acid made in the cytoplasm
moves into a mitochondrion.

36
00:01:50,324 --> 00:01:51,326
Zoom closer.

37
00:01:51,326 --> 00:01:54,933
Inside the mitochondrion is
a fluid called the matrix.

38
00:01:55,433 --> 00:01:57,060
Zoom into the matrix.

39
00:01:57,060 --> 00:02:01,076
Pyruvic acid loses one carbon
dioxide and joins coenzyme A.

40
00:02:01,076 --> 00:02:03,613
The enzyme is pyruvate dehydrogenase.

41
00:02:03,613 --> 00:02:06,762
This makes acetyl
coenzyme A, and one NADH.

42
00:02:07,412 --> 00:02:10,790
Now acetyl coenzyme A
enters the Krebs cycle.

43
00:02:10,790 --> 00:02:12,014
Step by step.

44
00:02:12,364 --> 00:02:13,264
Step one.

45
00:02:13,157 --> 00:02:17,520
Acetyl coenzyme A, with two carbons,
joins OAA, with four carbons.

46
00:02:17,520 --> 00:02:19,552
The enzyme is citrate synthase.

47
00:02:19,552 --> 00:02:22,280
This makes citric acid, with six carbons.

48
00:02:23,030 --> 00:02:23,930
Step two.

49
00:02:23,582 --> 00:02:25,410
Citric acid is rearranged into isocitrate.

50
00:02:26,160 --> 00:02:27,060
Step three.

51
00:02:27,030 --> 00:02:29,988
Two carbon dioxide leave,
one after the other.

52
00:02:29,988 --> 00:02:34,005
First alpha ketoglutaric acid
forms, and then succinyl coenzyme A.

53
00:02:34,755 --> 00:02:35,655
Step four.

54
00:02:35,315 --> 00:02:37,058
Succinyl coenzyme A becomes succinic acid.

55
00:02:37,058 --> 00:02:38,853
One GTP is made, and it makes one ATP.

56
00:02:39,603 --> 00:02:40,503
Step five.

57
00:02:40,313 --> 00:02:43,835
Succinic acid is changed back into
OAA, so the wheel turns again.

58
00:02:43,835 --> 00:02:47,700
On the way, three NAD plus become
NADH, and one FAD becomes FADH two.

59
00:02:48,450 --> 00:02:49,362
Remember this.

60
00:02:49,362 --> 00:02:50,547
OAA is made again.

61
00:02:50,547 --> 00:02:51,989
The cycle keeps turning.

62
00:02:52,589 --> 00:02:54,955
This wheel was worked out by Hans Krebs.

63
00:02:54,955 --> 00:02:58,700
It is also called the TCA cycle,
or the tricarboxylic acid cycle.

64
00:02:59,050 --> 00:03:03,288
Let's add up one pyruvic acid, from
entering the matrix to the end of one

65
00:03:03,288 --> 00:03:04,188
turn.

66
00:03:04,249 --> 00:03:07,602
Three carbon dioxide, four
NADH, one FADH two and one ATP.

67
00:03:07,602 --> 00:03:10,713
Almost all the energy is now
held in NADH and FADH two.

68
00:03:11,363 --> 00:03:13,719
Let's put the two paths side by side.

69
00:03:14,069 --> 00:03:17,455
Fermentation happens in the
cytoplasm, without oxygen.

70
00:03:17,455 --> 00:03:20,557
Pyruvic acid only becomes
ethanol or lactic acid.

71
00:03:20,557 --> 00:03:22,606
Very little energy is released.

72
00:03:23,056 --> 00:03:26,729
Aerobic respiration finishes in
the mitochondria, with oxygen.

73
00:03:26,729 --> 00:03:30,476
Pyruvic acid is broken down fully
to carbon dioxide and water.

74
00:03:30,476 --> 00:03:32,346
Much more energy is released.

75
00:03:32,946 --> 00:03:33,846
Your turn.

76
00:03:33,792 --> 00:03:35,846
Your legs burn after a sprint.

77
00:03:35,846 --> 00:03:38,805
Is it alcohol or lactic
acid in your muscles?

78
00:03:38,805 --> 00:03:40,051
Pause and decide.

79
00:03:43,251 --> 00:03:44,244
Lactic acid!

80
00:03:44,244 --> 00:03:48,136
Muscles short of oxygen turn
pyruvic acid into lactic acid.

81
00:03:48,786 --> 00:03:50,300
Now, a worked example.

82
00:03:50,300 --> 00:03:52,414
One glucose gives two pyruvic acid.

83
00:03:52,414 --> 00:03:54,448
What do both make in the matrix?

84
00:03:54,798 --> 00:03:55,875
Carbon dioxide.

85
00:03:55,875 --> 00:03:58,846
Three from each pyruvic
acid, so six in all.

86
00:03:59,296 --> 00:04:00,196
NADH.

87
00:03:59,757 --> 00:04:01,321
Four from each, so eight.

88
00:04:01,321 --> 00:04:03,569
And FADH two, one from each, so two.

89
00:04:04,019 --> 00:04:04,919
ATP.

90
00:04:04,487 --> 00:04:06,378
One from each turn, so two.

91
00:04:06,378 --> 00:04:09,911
Six carbon dioxide, eight
NADH, two FADH two, two ATP.

92
00:04:10,561 --> 00:04:12,025
Now, the exam trap.

93
00:04:12,375 --> 00:04:15,275
Many students think OAA is
used up in the Krebs cycle.

94
00:04:15,675 --> 00:04:18,441
But OAA is made again at
the end of every turn.

95
00:04:18,441 --> 00:04:20,113
That is why it is a cycle.

96
00:04:20,763 --> 00:04:22,000
Where is this asked?

97
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:25,629
As match questions on the enzymes
of fermentation and the Krebs cycle,

98
00:04:25,629 --> 00:04:27,514
and on the products of one turn.

99
00:04:28,114 --> 00:04:29,307
Exam level now.

100
00:04:29,307 --> 00:04:31,491
Match each enzyme with its job.

101
00:04:31,491 --> 00:04:32,735
Pause and solve.

102
00:04:38,135 --> 00:04:39,520
The answer is B.

103
00:04:39,520 --> 00:04:43,191
Pyruvic acid decarboxylase
works in yeast fermentation.

104
00:04:43,191 --> 00:04:47,034
Lactate dehydrogenase makes
lactic acid in tired muscles.

105
00:04:47,534 --> 00:04:49,735
Citrate synthase makes citric acid.

106
00:04:50,235 --> 00:04:53,103
And pyruvate dehydrogenase
makes acetyl coenzyme A.

107
00:04:53,703 --> 00:04:55,243
Here is your recap.

108
00:04:55,243 --> 00:04:56,826
Screenshot this card.

109
00:05:00,026 --> 00:05:02,212
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

110
00:05:02,612 --> 00:05:03,512
One.

111
00:05:02,986 --> 00:05:04,911
What does yeast make from pyruvic acid?

112
00:05:05,811 --> 00:05:06,711
Two.

113
00:05:06,310 --> 00:05:10,329
Which molecule joins acetyl
coenzyme A to start the Krebs cycle?

114
00:05:11,229 --> 00:05:12,129
Three.

115
00:05:11,644 --> 00:05:14,135
How many NADH come from one
pyruvic acid in the matrix?

116
00:05:15,235 --> 00:05:19,282
Next time: where do all these NADH
go, and how do they become ATP?

117
00:05:19,282 --> 00:05:21,910
We find out in the
electron transport system.
